  "id": "fig-231",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Illumination of the same photoresistor circuit as FIG230. Five incident-light arrows aim upwards and rightwards at the semiconductor. The unnumbered analog meter needle is nearly upright and slightly left of vertical, a larger deflection than the FIG230 upper-left low-side pointer. No numeric current, calibration, or temperature change is invented.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/00b1/lemmadeck/ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/9p/figures/fig-231.png",
      "sha256": "ea42450ee5178b937e1bc3b507e8b0a21eee771013dbf77fa8df4b15670f04f8"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "**热敏电阻**的电阻会随温度明显改变。\n本课讨论的这类半导体热敏元件，温度升高时电阻减小，温度降低时电阻增大。\n温度改变了材料中参与导电的电子和空穴的数量，电流通过的难易也随之改变。\n\n图 229 把热敏元件接入电路，用电流表观察电流的变化。\n保持电源电压不变，元件的电阻减小时，电路中的电流就增大。\n先标定不同温度下的读数，就可以由电流读数判断温度。\n\n其中感受温度的元件叫做**热敏电阻**，测温仪器还包括电源、读数装置等部分。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "半导体吸收射来的电磁辐射后会升温，电阻随温度改变，电路的读数也就改变。\n利用这种过程探测辐射的装置，叫做电阻辐射测温器，也叫辐射热计。\n原书用“能察觉距离几千米远的点燃火柴的辐射”说明历史上高灵敏装置的探测能力。\n\n这种灵敏度需要相应的装置和测量条件，实际探测还受辐射的收集、沿途传播和背景干扰影响。\n这不是每台辐射热计都有的固定检测距离。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "图 229"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "**光敏电阻**也利用半导体导电性可以改变的性质。\n半导体吸收光能后，导电性可以提高，电阻可以减小。\n这种光照引起的变化，在材料温度不变时也能发生。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "保持电源电压和半导体温度不变，先观察图 230 的电路。\n在图 231 中给半导体加上光照，电流表显示电流显著增大。\n电压相同而电流增大，说明半导体的导电性提高，电阻减小。\n\n因为温度保持不变，这次电流变化不能只用“光把材料加热了”来解释。\n光照产生导电变化与辐射先加热、再改变电阻，是两种需要区分的过程。\n实际光敏元件也可能受温度影响，不能把“温度不变时仍有光照效应”理解成“电阻永远不受温度影响”。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "Light"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "图 230"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "图 231"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "半导体吸收光的能量后，一些原先受束缚的电子能参与导电，原来的位置则留下空穴。\n自由电子能在电场作用下移动，传递电荷。\n空穴是少了一个电子的位置，不是材料中被挖出的洞。\n\n邻近电子可以填进这个位置，又在原来的位置留下空穴。\n这样接连填补时，空穴的位置也在移动，可以把这种导电视为正电荷的移动。\n电子和空穴都是载流子，也就是参与传递电荷的成员。\n\n光使参与导电的载流子增多，半导体的导电性就提高。\n原书把这种现象称为**光电效应**；\n在这里，具体表现是材料内部的光电导效应。\n\n它不要求电子飞出材料，也不要求先把整块材料加热。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "zh",
        "text": "利用这种光电导效应制成的电阻元件，叫做**光敏电阻**。\n它把光照变化转成电阻变化，再由电路转成电流读数的变化。\n原书指出这类元件轻巧、灵敏，可以用于不同技术领域的感光检测。\n\n具体元件的灵敏度和适用范围仍由材料、结构与工作条件决定。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "The resistance of a **thermistor** changes noticeably with temperature.\nFor the kind of semiconductor sensing element discussed here, warming lowers the resistance and cooling raises it.\nTemperature changes the number of electrons and holes that take part in conduction, so it changes how easily current flows.\n\nFigure 229 connects the temperature-sensitive element into a circuit and uses an ammeter to observe changes in current.\nWith the supply voltage held constant, a decrease in the element's resistance makes the circuit current increase.\nCalibrating the readings at different temperatures lets us use a current reading to determine the temperature.\n\nThe element that responds to temperature is the **thermistor**; the complete measuring instrument also includes a power supply and a readout."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "A semiconductor warms when it absorbs incoming electromagnetic radiation; its resistance changes with temperature, and the circuit reading changes too.\nAn instrument that detects radiation by this process is called a bolometer, or a resistance radiation thermometer.\nThe original textbook uses the claim that radiation from a burning match several kilometres away can be detected to illustrate the sensitivity of historical high-sensitivity instruments.\n\nSuch sensitivity requires a suitable instrument and measurement conditions; detection also depends on collecting the radiation, its transmission along the path, and background interference.\nIt is not a fixed detection distance that every bolometer can achieve."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "Figure 229"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "A **photoresistor** also uses the fact that a semiconductor's conductivity can change.\nWhen a semiconductor absorbs light energy, its conductivity can increase and its resistance can decrease.\nThis light-induced change can occur even when the material's temperature stays constant."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "Hold the supply voltage and the semiconductor's temperature constant, and first observe the Figure 230 circuit.\nIn Figure 231, illuminate the semiconductor; the ammeter shows a marked increase in current.\nAn increased current at the same voltage means that the semiconductor's conductivity has increased and its resistance has decreased.\n\nBecause the temperature stays constant, this current change cannot be explained simply by saying that light has warmed the material.\nA light-induced change in conduction and a resistance change caused by absorbed radiation first producing heat are distinct processes.\nReal light-sensitive elements can also respond to temperature: a light effect at constant temperature does not mean that their resistance can never depend on temperature."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "Light"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "Figure 230"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "Figure 231"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "When a semiconductor absorbs light energy, some previously bound electrons become able to conduct, leaving holes in their former places.\nFree electrons move under the influence of an electric field and carry charge.\nA hole is a place where an electron is missing, not a cavity cut into the material.\n\nA neighbouring electron can fill that place, leaving a hole where it came from.\nAs these places are filled in turn, the hole's position moves too; we can describe this conduction as the motion of positive charge.\nElectrons and holes are both charge carriers: they take part in transferring charge.\n\nLight increases the number of carriers available for conduction, increasing the semiconductor's conductivity.\nThe original textbook calls this the **photoelectric effect**;\nhere its particular form is photoconductivity within the material.\n\nIt does not require electrons to leave the material, or the whole material to be heated first."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s4-n113",
        "language": "en",
        "text": "A resistive element made to use this photoconductive effect is called a **photoresistor**.\nIt turns a change in illumination into a change in resistance, which the circuit then turns into a change in current reading.\nThe original textbook describes these elements as small and sensitive, suitable for detecting light in different technical applications.\n\nThe sensitivity and usable range of a particular element still depend on its material, structure, and operating conditions."
      }
    ],
